            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Which equation is the integrator-type cumulative rule taught in this step?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_0^t 2x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=3x(t)$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_0^t h(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=x(t-2)$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau\\right)$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Choice A is integrator-type because $y(t)$ is built by integrating past input values $x(\\tau)$ over a time-varying interval ending at $t$, which is exactly the cumulative rule this section trains.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For this step, which option is still the same cumulative-input pattern (with a controlled variation) and therefore belongs in the integrator-type family?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_{-2}^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_{t-1}^{t} x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_0^t x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\int_0^\\infty x(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=2x(t)-5$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Choice A keeps the same cumulative structure as the worked example: a fixed lower limit and the running upper limit $t$ with integrand $x(\\tau)$, so it is the same family with one controlled surface change.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Integrator-Type Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing a Pure Integrator with a Bounded Constant Input",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For the pure integrator $y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ with bounded constant input $x(t)=2$, pick the correct BIBO conclusion.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=2t$, which is unbounded, so the system is not BIBO stable.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\frac{2}{t}$, which stays bounded for all $t$, so the system is BIBO stable.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=2$, so the output is constant and bounded.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Because the input gain is only $2$, the system is stable regardless of growth.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=\\sin(2t)$, which is bounded, so the system is BIBO stable.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Substitute $x(\\tau)=2$ into the integral rule to get $y(t)=\\int_0^t 2\\,d\\tau =2t$. The output magnitude grows without bound as $t\\to\\infty$, so one bounded input produces an unbounded output and BIBO stability fails.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=\\int_{-1}^{t} x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and bounded constant input $x(t)=-4$, which result is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=-4(t+1)$ is unbounded, so the system is not BIBO stable.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=-4/t$ is bounded near $t=0$ and therefore BIBO stable.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y(t)=4(t+1)$ is bounded for all $t\\le 0$, so BIBO passes on the bounded-input check.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Because accumulation starts at $-1$, the output cannot grow without bound.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$x(t)=-4$ is unbounded, so this is not a valid BIBO test input.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The input is bounded because $x(t)=-4$ for all $t$. Integrating gives $y(t)=\\int_{-1}^t (-4)\\,d\\tau=-4(t+1)$, whose magnitude grows without bound as $t\\to\\infty$. A bounded input producing unbounded output means the system is not BIBO stable.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Integrator-Type Systems",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Testing a Scaled Integrator-Type System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=2\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and bounded $x(t)=1$, compute $y(t)$ and select the BIBO conclusion.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "y(t)=2\\int_0^t 1\\,d\\tau=2t",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
